// Winter, when you can see how a tree grew.
//
//     import 'trees' { Elm, Oak, Birch }
//
// A tree in leaf is mostly canopy: the wood is glimpsed between handfuls of green and its shape is
// taken on trust.  Strip the leaves and the shape is all there is, which is why this scene is set in
// the one season the library draws bare.
//
// **Nobody said how these branch.**  Each was grown into the room its crown was given: the room was
// scattered with points, and round after round every point drew the nearest twig a step toward it,
// until twigs had reached them all.  So a limb leaves its parent going one way and arrives somewhere
// else, bending as the points along its way pulled at it; branches crowd where the crown was full and
// stretch where it was thin; and no two forks divide alike.  Until 2026-10-22 these trees were built --
// a limb and three smaller limbs on its end, all the way down, each a curve bent by a rule -- and a
// winter stand of them read as a diagram: every fork of an oak dividing the way every other fork did.
//
// **How thick each limb is comes back from its twigs.**  Every twig ends as thick as every other, and
// where branches meet the wood below is as thick as those it feeds put together -- Leonardo's rule for
// trees, more or less.  It is why the trunks thicken toward the ground without anyone saying how much.
//
// **What a species is lies in its crown and in how far its points reach.**  The oak's points reach
// far, so a few long limbs sweep out through a crown of clumps; the birch's barely reach at all, so it
// divides finely and its twigs droop; the elm reaches up and out into a crown wider at the top.
//
// You will need the library, which is a thing to ask for once:
//
//     dotnet run -- libraries --install

import 'trees' { Elm, Oak, Birch }

context {
    parallel pixel scanner
    angles are degrees
    info {
        title 'Bare Wood'
        author 'J. Stephen Kress'
        description 'Winter trees, where the shape of the wood is all there is to look at.'
        copyright true
        software __software__
    }
}

camera {
    location [7.5, 5.2, -17]
    look at [-0.5, 4.6, 1]
    field of view 50.02
}

// A low sun almost behind them, so the limbs are drawn as edges rather than filled in -- which is
// what makes a curve read as a curve.
background physical sky {
    sun elevation 11
    sun azimuth 24
    turbidity 2.4
    brightness 3
}

sky light { samples 22 }

plane {
    material {
        pigment [0.55, 0.57, 0.60]
        ambient 0.16
        specular 0.03
    }
}

object Elm(9, 'winter', 4)    { translate [-5.6, 0, 2.5] }
object Oak(8, 'winter', 9)    { translate [0.4, 0, 0] }
object Birch(7.5, 'winter', 7) { translate [5.2, 0, -1.5] }
object Elm(7, 'winter', 12)   { translate [-10, 0, 6.5] }
